    $astroGA_master$> The revolution so far has been one of *ideas and calculation* — Copernicus's bold rearrangement, Kepler's laws wrung from Tycho's numbers. All of it happened in the mind and on the page; none of it could be *seen.* This chapter is about the moment the revolution became *visible* — when Galileo Galilei, in the winter of 1609–1610, pointed a new instrument, the **telescope**, at the sky and saw things no human being had ever seen. Mountains on the Moon, four moons circling Jupiter, the phases of Venus, spots on the Sun, the Milky Way dissolved into countless stars. These were not arguments but *evidence* — new facts the ancient cosmos could not accommodate. And alongside them Galileo began the **new physics of motion** — inertia — that finally answered the old objections to a moving Earth. This is where the revolution became something you could *see* through a glass, and where a new way of knowing, grounded in observation, laid its claim against the authority of tradition. It is the birth of the observational, and the modern, in astronomy.

In the autumn of 1609, in Padua, a forty-five-year-old professor of mathematics named Galileo Galilei heard a rumor: a Dutch spectacle-maker had built a curious tube with lenses that made distant things appear near. Galileo grasped the principle at once and — a superb craftsman as well as a brilliant mind — built his own, grinding and improving the lenses until he had an instrument magnifying some twenty or thirty times. And then he did something few if any had thought to do systematically before: instead of spotting ships at sea, **he pointed it at the sky.**

What followed was one of the most extraordinary episodes in the history of knowledge. Night after night, through that little tube, Galileo saw things no human eye had ever beheld — because no human eye had ever been *aided* to see them. He published what he saw in a slim, electrifying book in 1610, the *Sidereus Nuncius* — the "Starry Messenger" — and it caused a sensation across Europe. Here was something genuinely new: not a clever new *argument* about the cosmos, but new *facts*, direct observations revealing a heaven utterly unlike the one the ancients had described. And these facts did not sit quietly. They struck, one after another, at the foundations of the two-thousand-year-old geocentric cosmos.

{{image: Galileo Galilei | Galileo Galilei (1564–1642), who turned the newly invented telescope on the sky in 1609 and made the Copernican revolution something one could see. His discoveries — and his insistence that nature is known by observation, not authority — made him the father of observational astronomy and a father of modern physics.}}

## The shattering of the perfect heavens

Recall the Aristotelian worldview underlying the ancient cosmos: the universe was split into two utterly different realms. The *sublunary* realm (below the Moon) was the domain of change, decay, and ordinary rough matter; the *celestial* realm (the Moon and beyond) was one of *perfection* — flawless, eternal, unchanging spheres of a pure fifth element, moving in perfect circles. Galileo's telescope demolished this division, discovery by discovery.

He turned it on the **Moon** and saw not a smooth luminous orb but **mountains, craters, valleys, and plains** — a rugged, broken *landscape.* He watched the shadows of lunar mountains lengthen and shorten with the Sun's angle, exactly as on Earth, and from them even *estimated the mountains' heights.* The Moon is not a perfect celestial orb but a *world*, made of the same rough, ordinary stuff as the Earth. The sharp division between the perfect heavens and the corruptible Earth was false — a profound and unsettling demotion of the cosmos from perfection to ordinariness.

He turned it on the **Sun** (with proper caution) and found **spots** — dark blemishes that changed and moved across its face (revealing, we now know, that the Sun rotates). Even the Sun, supposedly the most perfect body of all, was blemished and changing. And he turned it on the **Milky Way** and saw that faint band **resolve into countless individual stars**, far too many to see with the naked eye — revealing a universe vastly larger and more populous than the cozy ancient sphere, and quietly bolstering the Copernican answer to the missing parallax: in a cosmos this vast, stars too far to shift are entirely plausible.

## A centre of motion that is not the Earth

The second great discovery struck geocentrism even more directly. Watching Jupiter over several nights, Galileo noticed four small "stars" in a line near it — and they *moved* from night to night, shifting sides, vanishing behind the planet and reappearing. He realized what they were: **four moons orbiting Jupiter**, the bodies we now call the Galilean moons. Watch them dance:

```jupitermoons
```

{{image: Galilean moons | The four Galilean moons — Io, Europa, Ganymede, and Callisto — shown to scale beside a sliver of Jupiter. Galileo saw them as faint points strung in a shifting line, and realized they were worlds orbiting Jupiter: the first bodies ever seen to circle a centre other than the Earth.}}

Why was this devastating? Because a bedrock assumption of the geocentric worldview was that **the Earth is the centre of *all* motion** — everything revolves around us. But here, plain in the glass, were four bodies revolving around *Jupiter.* There existed, unmistakably, a centre of motion that was *not* the Earth. If moons circle Jupiter, then it is simply false that everything circles the Earth. And it answered a specific objection raised against Copernicus: critics had argued that a moving Earth could never keep its Moon — surely a moving planet would leave its satellite behind? Jupiter refuted them: here was a planet indisputably in motion that *kept its four moons* as it went. **If Jupiter can move and keep its moons, so can the Earth.** The heavens themselves had answered the objection.

## The decisive test: the phases of Venus

Of all Galileo's discoveries, one stands apart as a genuine, clean observational *test* that geocentrism *failed* and heliocentrism *passed*: the **phases of Venus.** Watching Venus over months, Galileo saw it run through a *complete* cycle of phases, like the Moon — crescent, half, gibbous, and **full** — and, crucially, its apparent *size* swung enormously: large when a thin crescent, small when full. Slide Venus around its orbit and watch both the phase and the size change:

```venusphases
```

Here is the decisive point. The Ptolemaic model kept Venus's epicycle *between* the Earth and the Sun (that is how it reproduced the fact that Venus never strays far from the Sun). But if Venus always stays between us and the Sun, we only ever see its mostly-dark near side — so **the Ptolemaic model can produce only crescent and new phases; a full Venus is impossible**, because that would require Venus to be on the *far* side of the Sun, fully lit and facing us. Galileo saw a full Venus. The observation flatly contradicts the Ptolemaic prediction — pure geocentrism is *refuted, decisively, by the eye.* And what the full cycle *requires* is that **Venus orbits the Sun**: near and dark-sided (a large crescent) when it swings between us and the Sun, distant and full-faced (a small full disk) when it crosses to the far side. Work out that size swing yourself:

```example
venus-size
```

Let me be scrupulously honest, because it matters. The phases of Venus *decisively refute pure Ptolemaic geocentrism* — but they do *not*, by themselves, prove the full Copernican system, because they are *also* consistent with Tycho's hybrid model (in which Venus orbits the Sun but the Sun orbits a stationary Earth). So they prove that *Venus orbits the Sun* — killing pure geocentrism — without alone deciding between Copernican and Tychonic. **A single observation can decisively *refute* one theory while leaving others standing.** Fully establishing the moving Earth would need Newton's physics and, ultimately, the parallax measurement of 1838. Still, from 1610 onward the ancient cosmos was observationally *dead.*

## The instrument: extending the human eye

It is worth pausing on the *telescope itself*, because it was far more than a gadget — it was the first of a long line of instruments that would extend human senses beyond their natural limits. A simple refractor is two lenses: the large front *objective* gathers light and focuses it, and the small *eyepiece* magnifies the image. From this the telescope does two things the naked eye cannot. It **gathers far more light**: Galileo's 37-mm lens collected roughly 28 times more than the 7-mm pupil, so he could see the faint — the countless stars, the dim moons of Jupiter. And it **magnifies**, resolving detail invisible to the eye — the craters of the Moon, the disk and phases of Venus.

{{image: Sidereus Nuncius | A page from Galileo's Sidereus Nuncius (1610) showing his wash drawings of the Moon, its terminator jagged with mountains and pocked with craters. Direct observation, published for anyone to check, of a heaven utterly unlike the perfect crystalline spheres of ancient doctrine.}}

Here is the profound point, beyond the optics. For all of human history before 1609, everyone from the Babylonians to Tycho Brahe had seen the cosmos with the naked eye alone. Tycho's precision was superb, but it was the precision of the *eye.* **Galileo shattered that limit. He showed that by building an *instrument* one could see what had always been there but had always been invisible** — that the reach of human perception is not fixed by biology but can be *extended by technology.* The four moons of Jupiter had circled for billions of years; no human had ever seen them, not because they were hidden but because human eyes were too weak — until an instrument made them visible. This launched the entire program of instrument-driven astronomy that structures the rest of this course: bigger telescopes revealing fainter objects, the *spectroscope* decoding starlight into chemistry (the next chapter), radio and X-ray and space telescopes unveiling an invisible universe. **Every one is a descendant of Galileo's insight: build a better instrument, and see what was always there but beyond the unaided senses.**

There was a subtle problem Galileo had to confront, one that recurs throughout science: when you see something only *through an instrument*, how do you know it is real and not an artifact of the glass? Some contemporaries insisted the "moons of Jupiter" were illusions of the lenses. Galileo answered the way instrument science always must — by demonstrating *reliability*: the telescope shows real, checkable things when pointed at distant objects on Earth, and its revelations are *consistent* and *predictive* (the moons reappear where their orbits require; Venus's phases follow a regular cycle). An instrument earns trust by working, reliably and predictively, again and again — and establishing that instruments *can* be trusted was itself part of the birth of modern science.

## The new physics: answering the ancient objections

Galileo's discoveries were evidence *for* the new cosmos, but they did not by themselves answer the powerful *physical* objections against a moving Earth. If the Earth spins and hurtles through space, *why don't we feel it? Why don't dropped things fall behind? Why no perpetual gale?* In Aristotle's physics — still standard in Galileo's day — an object's natural state is *rest*; things move only while pushed, and stop when the push ceases. On that view, if the Earth moved, an unpushed stone should be *left behind* as the ground rushed east beneath it, landing far to the west; we should feel a constant wind. None of this happens, so the Earth must be still. These were not stupid objections — they followed rigorously from the accepted physics. To answer them, you had to overturn that physics.

Galileo did. His revolutionary insight — the seed of what Newton would call **inertia** — was that **an object in motion tends to *stay* in motion, all by itself, with no force required to keep it going.** Motion, contrary to Aristotle, needs no constant push to sustain. And from this follows the answer: **everything on the Earth *shares the Earth's motion* and keeps it, by inertia.** The stone, the bird, the air, you — all move *with* the Earth and continue to. So a dropped stone is *already moving eastward* with you and keeps that motion as it falls, landing right at your feet, not to the west. We feel no wind because the air moves with us, and *uniform* motion, shared by everything, produces no felt effect.

Galileo made this vivid with one of the most famous thought experiments in science — the **ship.** Imagine you are sealed below decks in a ship's cabin, sailing *smoothly and steadily* on a calm sea. *There is no experiment you can perform inside the cabin that will tell you whether the ship is moving or at rest.* A dropped ball falls straight to your feet; flies buzz, water drips, everything happens exactly as if the ship were docked — because everything shares the ship's motion. **Uniform motion is undetectable from within.** This is the principle of **Galilean relativity**, and it is the complete and devastating answer to "we would feel the Earth move": *no, we would not*, because a smoothly moving Earth feels exactly like a still one. The single most powerful common-sense argument against Copernicus was simply *wrong*, and Galileo showed precisely why.

This new physics was as important as the telescope — in some ways more. The discoveries provided *evidence* against the old cosmos; the physics of inertia removed the *conceptual obstacle* that had made a moving Earth seem impossible. Galileo made the moving Earth not just astronomically attractive but *physically permissible* — and by replacing Aristotle's physics of motion with the beginnings of a physics of inertia, he laid the groundwork for Newton, who would complete it. His studies of falling bodies (all fall at the same rate, ignoring air resistance) and projectile motion were part of the same revolution. **Galileo was the father of observational astronomy and a father of modern physics — the science of motion that made the moving Earth intelligible.**

## The trial: evidence against authority

We cannot tell Galileo's story without the trial — but it must be told *honestly*, because the popular version (brave lone scientist versus benighted anti-science church) is a caricature that obscures the real, more instructive story. The sequence, briefly: his discoveries made heliocentrism a live public issue; in 1616 the Church declared the doctrine contrary to scripture and warned Galileo not to hold or defend it as literally true; in 1632 he published his *Dialogue Concerning the Two Chief World Systems*, a brilliant, thinly veiled case *for* heliocentrism (and, unwisely, put the Pope's preferred argument in the mouth of a character named Simplicio — "the simpleton"); in 1633 the Roman Inquisition tried him, found him "vehemently suspect of heresy," forced him to *recant*, and sentenced him to house arrest until his death in 1642.

Why is the cartoon of "pure science crushed by pure superstition" misleading? For several reasons worth being honest about. **Galileo was himself a devout Catholic**, not an enemy of religion; he believed heliocentrism compatible with scripture properly read. **The evidence was not yet conclusive** — this is crucial and often forgotten. The phases of Venus refuted *pure* geocentrism but were consistent with Tycho's hybrid; stellar parallax was still unseen (the direct proof would not come until 1838); and there was as yet no *physics* proving the Earth moves (Newton was decades away). So a cautious, even scientifically informed person in 1633 could reasonably hold that heliocentrism was *not yet proven*, and that Galileo overstated his case by insisting on it as established fact. **And politics and personality mattered enormously** — the trial came during the Counter-Reformation, when the Church was acutely sensitive to challenges to its authority, and Galileo had personally antagonized Pope Urban VIII, once his admirer.

So the trial was not simply science versus religion. But it *was* about something profound, and here the popular story captures a real truth: **it was a collision between two ways of knowing — the authority of tradition and scripture, versus the evidence of observation and reason.** At its core lay the question: *who decides what is true about the natural world?* The Church claimed the authority to determine truth about nature by scripture and tradition; Galileo claimed that truth about nature must be decided by *observation and evidence* — that "the book of nature is written in the language of mathematics" and read by looking, and that no authority, however revered, can overrule what careful observation reveals. **This methodological claim — that evidence, not authority, is the final judge of truth about nature — is the deep issue of the trial, and Galileo's deepest legacy.** Even if his specific case was not yet fully proven, his *principle* is the foundation of science. The Church won the battle — Galileo recanted — but lost the war: the principle he championed became the method of modern science, and the moving Earth he was forced to deny is now simply known to be true. (In 1992 the Vatican formally acknowledged the Church had erred.) He was made to kneel, but the telescope had already shown what it had shown, and no recantation could un-see it.

## The revolution you could see

Gather the argument, because this chapter is where the revolution became *visible* — a matter of evidence anyone could check by looking through a glass. Galileo built his own telescope in 1609 and pointed it, systematically, at the heavens, publishing what no eye had ever seen in the *Sidereus Nuncius.* *Mountains and craters on the Moon* revealed it a rugged *world* of ordinary stuff, destroying the doctrine of celestial perfection. *Four moons circling Jupiter* proved the Earth is not the centre of all motion, and that a moving planet keeps its moons. *Sunspots* showed even the Sun blemished and changing; the *Milky Way resolved into countless stars* revealed a vastly larger universe. The decisive blow was the *phases of Venus* — a complete cycle, crescent large and full small, impossible in the Ptolemaic model and requiring Venus to orbit the Sun. It refuted pure geocentrism decisively (though, honestly, it fit Tycho's hybrid too). And his *new physics of inertia and the ship* answered the ancient objections at last: a smoothly moving Earth carries everything with it and feels exactly like a still one, so our feeling no motion is no evidence at all.

**Here is the lesson to carry.** Galileo turned the revolution from calculation into *sight* — from something argued in the study to something *seen* through a glass, by anyone who would look. And in doing so he asserted the founding principle of modern science: that the truth about nature is decided by *observation and evidence*, not by authority, tradition, or text — that we must *look*, and let nature be the judge. This is the course's through-line at its clearest: reading the true structure of the cosmos from the appearances demands, above all, the willingness to *observe* — to build the best eye we can, look at what is actually there, and trust what we see over what we have been told. **He was the father of observational astronomy and a father of modern physics — but his deepest legacy is the principle that evidence, not authority, is the final judge of truth about nature.** That principle, more than any single discovery, is the engine of everything astronomy has learned since. He was made to kneel, but the sky he had seen through the glass was the true one, and it has been ours ever since. The revolution now has its bold idea, its precise laws, and its decisive evidence and new physics. One thing remains: the *cause* — why the planets move as they do — and for that we turn, next, to Newton.

## Further reading

Galileo's own *Sidereus Nuncius* (1610) is short and thrilling, and his *Dialogue Concerning the Two Chief World Systems* (1632) is surprisingly readable — witty, vivid, and combative. For the trial, any careful modern history (there are many) will show how much more tangled and human the story is than the legend; and the 1992 Vatican statement is worth reading as a coda.$astroGA_master$,
